1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:01,702
Section one.

2
00:00:01,982 --> 00:00:07,572
Before writing any dbt model, we need data in the database, and we need to understand it.

3
00:00:07,852 --> 00:00:14,700
We load two months of real taxi trips into DuckDB with the agent, and then we find out what is wrong with the data.

4
00:00:15,799 --> 00:00:20,663
Here is something that surprises many beginners. dbt does not load data.

5
00:00:20,944 --> 00:00:23,653
It does not download files or call APIs.

6
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:26,984
It transforms data that is already in your database.

7
00:00:27,263 --> 00:00:30,741
Loading is done by other tools, before dbt starts.

8
00:00:31,841 --> 00:00:35,105
Extract: the Taxi Commission publishes Parquet files.

9
00:00:35,385 --> 00:00:38,585
Load: DuckDB reads them into a schema called raw.

10
00:00:38,865 --> 00:00:42,257
Transform: that is dbt, and the rest of this course.

11
00:00:42,537 --> 00:00:48,553
We keep the loading SQL in a script, outside dbt, so we can run it again when a new month arrives.

12
00:00:49,653 --> 00:00:52,319
The dbt introduction says the same thing.

13
00:00:52,599 --> 00:01:01,303
Data is extracted and loaded by other tools. dbt handles the transformation: modelling, cleaning, testing and documenting.

14
00:01:01,583 --> 00:01:06,618
And because dbt only writes select statements, the database does the heavy work.

15
00:01:07,718 --> 00:01:08,721
The dataset.

16
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:13,054
Parquet files, one per month, around three to four million trips each.

17
00:01:13,334 --> 00:01:19,777
We start with November and December twenty twenty-four, and add January twenty twenty-five later, on purpose.

18
00:01:20,057 --> 00:01:23,812
There is also a zone lookup file and an official data dictionary.

19
00:01:24,911 --> 00:01:26,661
Each row is one taxi trip.

20
00:01:26,941 --> 00:01:31,592
When and where: pickup and dropoff times and zones, distance and passengers.

21
00:01:31,871 --> 00:01:36,330
Codes, like rate code and payment type, that only make sense with the dictionary.

22
00:01:36,610 --> 00:01:37,762
And money columns.

23
00:01:38,042 --> 00:01:43,034
The last one, the congestion fee, only exists from January twenty twenty-five.

24
00:01:44,134 --> 00:01:45,649
Our first habit in action.

25
00:01:45,929 --> 00:01:51,262
We transcribe the official data dictionary into a Markdown file inside the project.

26
00:01:51,542 --> 00:01:59,371
From now on, the agent reads code lists and descriptions from that file, not from memory, because lists like these change over time.

27
00:02:00,472 --> 00:02:02,370
This is the first prompt for the demo.

28
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:11,269
In the SQL editor, the agent creates a raw schema and loads both monthly files into one table, keeping the file name on every row.

29
00:02:11,549 --> 00:02:16,797
We ask to see the SQL first, and Rosetta stops at an approval gate before anything runs.

30
00:02:17,897 --> 00:02:22,398
This is roughly the SQL the agent writes, and we save it as the load script.

31
00:02:22,678 --> 00:02:26,305
One create table statement that reads both Parquet files.

32
00:02:26,585 --> 00:02:32,942
Union by name lets files with different columns combine, which matters later when a new column arrives.

33
00:02:34,042 --> 00:02:42,639
Next, in Ask mode, the agent profiles the table: rows per file, the date range, empty values per column, and three suspicious things.

34
00:02:42,919 --> 00:02:47,719
Negative fares, trips with zero distance, and trips that end before they start.

35
00:02:47,999 --> 00:02:50,517
We look at the data before we write rules about it.

36
00:02:51,617 --> 00:02:53,579
Here is what real data looks like.

37
00:02:53,859 --> 00:02:56,164
Trips picked up outside the month of their file.

38
00:02:56,443 --> 00:02:57,531
Negative fares.

39
00:02:57,811 --> 00:02:59,326
Trips with zero distance.

40
00:02:59,606 --> 00:03:01,910
A block of rows with no passenger count.

41
00:03:02,190 --> 00:03:04,131
And trips that end before they start.

42
00:03:04,411 --> 00:03:05,734
This is not our bug.

43
00:03:06,014 --> 00:03:07,145
This is the source.

44
00:03:08,245 --> 00:03:11,082
Every one of these problems has a home in this course.

45
00:03:11,362 --> 00:03:13,197
Guessed tests in the sources section.

46
00:03:13,477 --> 00:03:15,183
Changed code lists in seeds.

47
00:03:15,463 --> 00:03:17,213
Backwards trips in data tests.

48
00:03:17,493 --> 00:03:20,949
Zero distance and invalid trips in the intermediate model.

49
00:03:21,229 --> 00:03:24,194
And the new January column in incremental models.
